ock contains more than one of the first $t$ integers. We give a closed formula for the number of multi-chains of such non-crossing partitions with prescribed number of blocks. Building on this result, we compute Chapoton's $M$-triangle in this setting and conjecture a combinatorial interpretation for the $H$-triangle. This conjecture is proved for $m=1$.\n\n  * $k$-Indivisible Noncrossing Partitions\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSearch | arXiv e-print repository (https://accelresearching.com/accel-zinc-picolinate-50mg/?_=%2Fsearch%2F%23nLw79nwZb1b3uPkZUsgpm5M%3D&query=Krattenthaler%2C+C&searchtype=author&start=0)\nciteturn6search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago;   28. arXiv:1910.13244  [pdf, ps, other] ... Building on this result, we compute Chapoton's $M$-triangle in this setting and conjecture a combinatorial interpretation for the $H$-triangle.\n\n  28. arXiv:1910.13244  [pdf, ps, other] \n\nmath.CO\n\ndoi 10.5802/alco.219\n\nThe Rank Enumeration of Certain Parabolic Non-Crossing Partitions\n\nAuthors: Christian Krattenthaler, Henri Mühle\n\nAbstract: We consider $m$-divisible non-crossing partitions of $\\{1,2,\\ldots,mn\\}$ with the property that for some $t\\leq n$ no block contains more than one of the first $t$ integers. We give a closed formula for the number of multi-chains of such non-crossing partitions with prescribed number of blocks. Building on this result, we compute Chapoton's $M$-triangle in this setting and conjecture a combinatori… ▽ More We consider $m$-divisible non-crossing partitions of $\\{1,2,\\ldots,mn\\}$ with the property that for some $t\\leq n$ no block contains more than one of the first $t$ integers. We give a closed formula for the number of multi-chains of such non-crossing partitions with prescribed number of blocks. Building on this result, we compute Chapoton's $M$-triangle in this setting and conjecture a combinatorial interpretation for the $H$-triangle. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTop arXiv papers (https://scirate.com/?date=2025-09-03&page=145&range=31)\nciteturn6search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.2 years ago; Crawled: 2 days ago; Aug 26 2025 math.CO arXiv:2508.17854v3 ... @misc{2508.17854, author = {Gaurav Kottari and Niteesh Sahni}, title = {{S}tructural {C}haracterisations of (n-1,n)-{T}rees}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2508.17854}, note = {arXiv:2508.17854v3} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\n  * A new spin on polynomial relations among kappa classes\n\nAlexander Alexandrov, Boris Bychkov, Petr Dunin-Barkowski, Maxim Kazarian, Sergey Shadrin\n\nAug 26 2025 math.AG hep-th math-ph math.MP arXiv:2508.17865v3\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2508.17865, author = {Alexander Alexandrov and Boris Bychkov and Petr Dunin-Barkowski and Maxim Kazarian and Sergey Shadrin}, title = {{A} new spin on polynomial relations among kappa classes}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2508.17865}, note = {arXiv:2508.17865v3} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nWe prove a recent conjecture of the fourth named author with P. Norbury that states a system of universal polynomial relations among the kappa classes on the moduli spaces of algebraic curves. The proof involves localization and materialization analysis of the spin Gromov-Witten theory of the projective line and is dictated by $\\mathbb{Z}_2$-equivariant topological recursion.\n\n  * Symmetry Classification of Altermagnetism and Emergence of Type-IV Magnetism in Two Dimensions\n\nMu Tian, Chaoxi Cui, Zeying Zhang, Jingyi Duan, Wanxiang Feng, Run-Wu Zhang\n\nAug 26 2025 cond-mat.mtrl-sci cond-mat.str-el arXiv:2508.17864v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2508.17864, author = {Mu Tian and Chaoxi Cui and Zeying Zhang and Jingyi Duan and Wanxiang Feng and Run-Wu Zhang}, title = {{S}ymmetry {C}lassification of {A}ltermagnetism and {E}mergence of {T}ype-{IV} {M}agnetism in {T}wo {D}imensions}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2508.17864}, note = {arXiv:2508.17864v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nTwo-dimensional (2D) magnetism, particularly 2D altermagnetism (AM), has attracted considerable interest due to its exceptional physical properties and broad application potential. However, the classification of AM undergoes a fundamental paradigm shift when transitioning from three-dimensional (3D) to 2D symmetry-enforced fully compensated collinear magnetism$-$a shift that has remained largely overlooked. \nHaijiang Liu, Qiyuan Li, Chao Gao, Yong Cao, Xiangyu Xu, Xun Wu, Daniel Hershcovich, Jinguang Gu\n\nAug 26 2025 cs.CL cs.CY arXiv:2508.17855v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2508.17855, author = {Haijiang Liu and Qiyuan Li and Chao Gao and Yong Cao and Xiangyu Xu and Xun Wu and Daniel Hershcovich and Jinguang Gu}, title = {{B}eyond {D}emographics: {E}nhancing {C}ultural {V}alue {S}urvey {S}imulation with {M}ulti-{S}tage {P}ersonality-{D}riven {C}ognitive {R}easoning}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2508.17855}, note = {arXiv:2508.17855v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nIntroducing MARK, the Multi-stAge Reasoning frameworK for cultural value survey response simulation, designed to enhance the accuracy, steerability, and interpretability of large language models in this task. The system is inspired by the type dynamics theory in the MBTI psychological framework for personality research. It effectively predicts and utilizes human demographic information for simulation: life-situational stress analysis, group-level personality prediction, and self-weighted cognitive imitation. Experiments on the World Values Survey show that MARK outperforms existing baselines by 10% accuracy and reduces the divergence between model predictions and human preferences. This highlights the potential of our framework to improve zero-shot personalization and help social scientists interpret model predictions.\n\n  * Structural Characterisations of (n-1,n)-Trees\n\nGaurav Kottari, Niteesh Sahni\n\nAug 26 2025 math.CO arXiv:2508.17854v3\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2508.17854, author = {Gaurav Kottari and Niteesh Sahni}, title = {{S}tructural {C}haracterisations of (n-1,n)-{T}rees}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2508.17854}, note = {arXiv:2508.17854v3} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nWe study higher-dimensional analogues of graph-theoretic trees within the class of pure n-simplicial complexes. Focusing on the case m = n-1 in Dewdney's (m, n)-tree framework, we introduce refined notions of path and circuit sequences that overcome the structural limitations of existing definitions. Using these refinements, we establish higher-dimensional analogues of the classical characterisations of trees in graphs, including equivalences based on connectivity, acyclicity, path uniqueness, and enumerative constraints. We further disprove two conjectures posed by Dewdney by constructing explicit counterexamples, and we formulate corrected versions that hold under additional necessary conditions in the case m = n-1. These results provide a structural characterisation of (n-1, n)-trees, parallel to the classical theory of graph-theoretic trees.\n\n  * Box-Level Class-Balanced Sampling for Active Object Detection\n\nJingyi Liao, Xun Xu, Chuan-Sheng Foo, Lile Cai\n\nAug 26 2025 cs.CV arXiv:2508.17849v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2508.17849, author = {Jingyi Liao and Xun Xu and Chuan-Sheng Foo and Lile Cai}, title = {{B}ox-{L}evel {C}lass-{B}alanced {S}ampling for {A}ctive {O}bject {D}etection}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2508.17849}, note = {arXiv:2508.17849v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nTraining deep object detectors demands expensive bounding box annotation. Active learning (AL) is a promising technique to alleviate the annotation burden. Performing AL at box-level for object detection, i.e., selecting the most informative boxes to label and supplementing the sparsely-labelled image with pseudo labels, has been shown to be more cost-effective than selecting and labelling the entire image. In box-level AL for object detection, we observe that models at early stage can only perform well on majority classes, making the pseudo labels severely class-imbalanced. We propose a class-balanced sampling strategy to select more objects from minority classes for labelling, so as to make the final training data, \\ie, ground truth labels obtained by AL and pseudo labels, more class-balanced to train a better model. We also propose a task-aware soft pseudo labelling strategy to increase the accuracy of pseudo labels. We evaluate our method on public benchmarking datasets and show that our method achieves state-of-the-art performance.\n\n  * Constraining deviations from $\\Lambda$CDM in the Hubble expansion rate\n\nYupeng Yang\n\nAug 26 2025 astro-ph.CO arXiv:2508.17848v3\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2508.17848, author = {Yupeng Yang}, title = {{C}onstraining deviations from $\\{L}ambda${CDM} in the {H}ubble expansion rate}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2508.17848}, doi = {10.1140/epjc/s10052-025-15088-8}, note = {arXiv:2508.17848v3} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\n\n  * Diffusion-Based Data Augmentation for Medical Image Segmentation\n\nMaham Nazir, Muhammad Aqeel, Francesco Setti\n\nAug 26 2025 cs.CV cs.LG arXiv:2508.17844v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2508.17844, author = {Maham Nazir and Muhammad Aqeel and Francesco Setti}, title = {{D}iffusion-{B}ased {D}ata {A}ugmentation for {M}edical {I}mage {S}egmentation}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2508.17844}, note = {arXiv:2508.17844v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nMedical image segmentation models struggle with rare abnormalities due to scarce annotated pathological data. We propose DiffAug a novel framework that combines textguided diffusion-based generation with automatic segmentation validation to address this challenge. Our proposed approach uses latent diffusion models conditioned on medical text descriptions and spatial masks to synthesize abnormalities via inpainting on normal images. Generated samples undergo dynamic quality validation through a latentspace segmentation network that ensures accurate localization while enabling single-step inference. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSearch | arXiv e-print repository (https://accelresearching.com/accel-zinc-picolinate-50mg/?_=%2Fsearch%2F%23nLw79nwZb1b3uPkZUsgpm5M%3D&query=Krattenthaler%2C+C&searchtype=author)\nciteturn6search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; We do this in two ways: (1) by using modular function theory, and (2) by applying the Weierstraßaddition formula for theta products. ...   28. arXiv:1910.13244  [pdf, ps, other]\nIn particular, we prove corresponding conjectures of the second author. We show that, by means of the Jacobi triple product identity, all these generating functions can be reduced to a linear combination of theta function products. The proof of our formulae then consists in simplifying these linear combinations of theta products into single products. We do this in two ways: (1) by using modular function theory, and (2) by applying the Weierstraßaddition formula for theta products. △ Less\n\nSubmitted 7 August, 2021; v1 submitted 10 December, 2019; originally announced December 2019.\n\nComments: AnS-LaTeX, 38 pages; some typos corrected\n\nMSC Class: Primary 11B65; Secondary 05A30; 11F27; 33D05\n\nJournal ref: in: Transcendence in Algebra, Combinatorics, Geometry and Number Theory, A. Bostan, K. Raschel (eds.), Springer-Verlag, 2021, pp. 193-236\n\n  28. arXiv:1910.13244  [pdf, ps, other] \n\nmath.CO\n\ndoi 10.5802/alco.219\n\nThe Rank Enumeration of Certain Parabolic Non-Crossing Partitions\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRESEARCH STATEMENTHENRI MÜHLEBACKGROUNDCombinatorial families hardly ever arise as plain sets. Usually, they are naturally equipped with (https://hmuehle.github.io/docs/researc